Hmm, very interesting to see this thread on RGRN. In short, different
methods of compiling, different C libraries, and different
compile-time options do have a an effect on RNG. I have found this out
after a little bit of research into a possible way of cheating at
Nethack on an online server, which I will now explain.

I have recently been thinking about the possibilities that come from
the fact that games started at exactly the same time (to within a
second) will have the same random seed. Specifically, this fact could
be used to cheat. You could predict what you would find in a game
started at a specific time, at least as far as the starting inventory,
character stats, and first level. You could also search for a "good"
time to play: good in the sense that you start with very useful items
(charm monster and / of polymorph?).

Having done some tests, I think that this kind of cheating is very
possible provided that you can get an accurate idea of what the time
is on the Nethack server, and that you can get a copy of the random
number generator used by the server, and that you know what
compile-time options were used.

The first problem is not hard because Nethack servers provide game
recording, and timestamp recordings with their start time. The second
problem is also potentially easy because the RNG used by Nethack is
built into the system C library: all you need is the same C library.
And as to the third problem: well, #version will be useful!

To avoid this, I suggest that future versions of Nethack should get
their random seeds from /dev/random or /dev/urandom if either is
available, and default to the system time if not. That way, the seed
would be entirely unpredictable on most Linux systems at least.

